Janne writes: 
>After I posted my question about The vibrating square (answer quick pls,
>Im dying to go on) I thought about it some more. How on earth is it pos-
>sible to understand that one should find it WITHOUT reading spoilers??

You reach a level with no more steps down.  There's a vibrating square
there.  Therefore, you need to use the square.  You have a bell, book,
and candle, and have either read literature or played Zork I.  Thus
you know what to do.  All possible, in theory.

>I guess that the oracle might tell me what I need to know in this game
>BUT when you pass level 6 you normally dont have that much money to ask
>him more than once or twice. (I think its to long to walk back to ask now.)
>Im not sure how the code works but IF I should try I belive that the oracle
>will answer a lot of stuff that is not new to me, right? (About the Wiz,
>about a wand of wishes in a castle etc etc)

Invoke a quest artifact to level teleport to the oracle level when you 
have lots of money.  Or read a scroll of teleport while confused with 
teleport control.  Or, if you're cheap, you can get the source and
read the unencrypted rumor file. 

>Has anyone tried out the oracle completely? Is it worth the prize and effort?
>Will he answer randomly or when I get one answer, I wont get that answer again
>during that game? (Ofcourse I mean the expensive questions, not the cheap one)

Not worth it.  I haven't got a repeat on expensive questions, but I haven't
asked them all, either.  And there's a comment in the code about 
possible reuse of rumors (cheap questions / graffiti / fortune cookies),
but I don't think it applies to the expensive ones.

>Spoil me, pls (but first answer EXACT location of The VS on last level).

Polymorph into a Xorn and cover every single square on the level.  That's
how I found it last time.  (It's too much of a pain to walk the halls.)
Magic mapping can be a bad idea sometimes, because you forget where you
have and haven't been and end up running around in circles.  Last 
time I played, the vibrating square was about halfway down the screen
and slightly to the left of center, in a passageway.

